Subject: [PATCH 09/12] Plantard NTT: record the Path A dead-end and reset to
 Path B baseline
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Attempted Path A (signed Plantard centred-form in plant_18433.c) and
hit a bug I could not localize in one iteration:

  * Single-coefficient plant_red() matches mq18433_montymul over many
    random inputs (signed and unsigned, including products in [-Q^2,
    Q^2]).
  * 2-layer NTT (n=4) matches reference byte-for-byte after normalise.
  * 8-layer NTT (n=256) diverges: ~12 positions off by exactly 1 at
    layer 1, growing to ~all 256 by layer 6, with diffs that aren't
    multiples of Q (i.e. not just different representatives — actually
    different modular values).
  * Switching plant_red to unsigned arithmetic (= literally
    Zq(montyred)) does NOT fix it, so the bug is in
    centred_reduce / cell-cast / to_canonical interaction, not in the
    reducer itself.

Without an ARM toolchain and on-target verification to corroborate, I
can't safely iterate further on speculative code. Reverted plant_18433.c
to the byte-identical-by-construction baseline; cross-check passes
trivially.

PLANTARD_NOTES.md now documents:
  * What the Path A attempt was structurally.
  * Where the failure surfaced and what it looked like.
  * Two concrete experiments to disambiguate signed-Plantard
    incompatibility vs. a bug in the cast/normalise plumbing.

The C 'plant' path is effectively Path B now (canonical-form math
mirroring the reference); the byte contract still holds; the asm port
remains the next milestone, with the test_plant_ntt cross-check as the
regression net once an ARM environment is available.
